(b) C 1s XPS, (c) N 1s XPS, and (d) O 1s
XPS of candle soot (CS), activated candle soot (ACS), and activated
N-doped candle soot (ANCS) catalysts.</p></div></div></div><div id="fig5" content-id="fig5" class="fig fig-modal reveal-modal" data-reveal="data-reveal"><div class="fig-label fig-label">Figure 5</div><div class="graphic-wrap"><a class="fig-link" href="/view-large/figure/165496540/ao4c03987_0005.tif" target="_blank" rel="nofollow" aria-describedby="sr-fig-viewer-action"><img class="content-image js-graphic-position-float" src="https://acs.silverchair-cdn.com/acs/content_public/journal/acsodf/9/33/10.1021_acsomega.4c03987/3/m_ao4c03987_0005.png?Expires=1791347537&amp;Signature=eBv9jXhyvPOxKHF4hwnRkmwirgH0TOLbZJM1rYi-8lDK67o3vHUm04MAC-lBgAfvyZrv5GgsTowtQC6N3isXjKxS0HZCWpqmsic0afomjQFR~rYrXG7EpfVDnFbeA45uelFef961aSFFo5tyQUcdR1mG59lI-k12vuZd706DLJJX-jpXDxSwRusFqqTyahOi-3BQCu5olgKgvb-50q0YkQZWoh2GNGhtQkTdl4AIrcZb2DYkM1is5XAdxlcABn~iLSfjvD6UYiBD0KBJx5nDZKxJHlr~W5OcLZuhtrfrTlaoRPr6Fz~dR0D4qKIqvGU4OiZduJH-n53MV750YXmPRw__&amp;Key-Pair-Id=APKAIE5G5CRDK6RD3PGA" data-src="https://acs.silverchair-cdn.com/acs/content_public/journal/acsodf/9/33/10.1021_acsomega.4c03987/3/m_ao4c03987_0005.png?Expires=1791347537&amp;Signature=eBv9jXhyvPOxKHF4hwnRkmwirgH0TOLbZJM1rYi-8lDK67o3vHUm04MAC-lBgAfvyZrv5GgsTowtQC6N3isXjKxS0HZCWpqmsic0afomjQFR~rYrXG7EpfVDnFbeA45uelFef961aSFFo5tyQUcdR1mG59lI-k12vuZd706DLJJX-jpXDxSwRusFqqTyahOi-3BQCu5olgKgvb-50q0YkQZWoh2GNGhtQkTdl4AIrcZb2DYkM1is5XAdxlcABn~iLSfjvD6UYiBD0KBJx5nDZKxJHlr~W5OcLZuhtrfrTlaoRPr6Fz~dR0D4qKIqvGU4OiZduJH-n53MV750YXmPRw__&amp;Key-Pair-Id=APKAIE5G5CRDK6RD3PGA" alt="Figure 5. Refer to the image caption for details." path-from-xml="ao4c03987_0005.tif" /></a><div class="fig-orig original-slide"><a section="165496540" class="fig-view-orig at-figureViewLarge" role="button" href="/view-large/figure/165496540/ao4c03987_0005.tif" path-from-xml="ao4c03987_0005.tif" target="_blank" rel="nofollow" aria-label="View large Figure 5.">View Large</a><a section="165496540" href="//acs.silverchair-cdn.com/DownloadFile/DownloadImage.aspx?image=https://acs.silverchair-cdn.com/acs/content_public/journal/acsodf/9/33/10.1021_acsomega.4c03987/3/ao4c03987_0005.png?Expires=1791347537&Signature=enFGos5~3LaqftyFm~qADD5UgqwCuH1p1hACKbpc2HGsS2S-BZAGtHBVCv0jLEH~Dl2qoPXeJoRU5uq9eFib0bbEV9iZEEa6PY-rkwTPnPMwGRIiBdCThsFjdVeSqssA0SueV0mYxZlopK7rW9zFywGe9G9ygwPE48Q7SG41vjvZeE9w0ZRuiH3dUuwz3Y1ohEGtEaXQyxjnXtAkVRXp2dhLit3prF44Yze8ZigLnUnOGizh006SLh2faG8-8bIgxgoGLzeonPdf0NZ9T1zfUNQRkAlVnqVbl4iZwACkFzhZY41DmBeMqlEnh0u3E4nTmVLZxmdZow2YMrhOGfwoMg__&Key-Pair-Id=APKAIE5G5CRDK6RD3PGA&sec=165496540&ar=358560&xsltPath=~/UI/app/XSLT&imagename=&siteId=1000073" class="download-slide stats-download-slide js-download-file-gtm-datalayer-event" role="button" data-filetype="ppt" path-from-xml="ao4c03987_0005.tif" target="_blank" rel="nofollow" aria-label="Download slide for Figure 5.">Download to Slide</a></div></div><div class="graphic-bottom"><div class="label fig-label">Figure 5.</div><div class="caption fig-caption"><p>(a) FT-IR spectra and
(b) C 1s XPS, (c) N 1s XPS, and (d) O 1s
XPS of candle soot (CS), activated candle soot (ACS), and activated
N-doped candle soot (ANCS) catalysts.</p></div></div><a class="close-reveal-modal" href="javascript:;"><i class="icon-general-close" aria-hidden="true"> </i><span class="screenreader-text">Close Figure 5.</span></a></div>
                </div>
                <div id="165496541-content" class="article-section-wrapper js-article-section js-content-section  " data-section-parent-id="165496533">
                    
                    <p>The XPS analysis was conducted to elucidate the nature of carbon,
nitrogen, and oxygen species in the carbon catalysts. The C 1s XP
spectrum of pure CS shows three major peaks at the binding energies
(BEs) of 284.2, 285.7, and 289.6 eV, corresponding to the carbon species
of C=C, C–O, and O–C=O groups, respectively (<a href="javascript:;" reveal-id="fig5" data-open="fig5" class="link link-reveal link-table xref-fig">Figure 5</a>b). (<a href="javascript:;" ref-data-modal-source-id="ref30" class="link link-ref xref-bibr js-link-ref">30</a>) The close observation of <a href="javascript:;" reveal-id="fig5" data-open="fig5" class="link link-reveal link-table xref-fig">Figure 5</a>b reveals that the major C 1s peak (284.2
eV) belonging to C=C in the CS sample is shifted to higher BEs in
the ACS and ANCS samples, whereas the C 1s peaks of the C–O
and O–C=O groups are shifted to lower BEs. The ANCS sample
exhibits an additional peak at 292.2 eV, corresponding to carbon species
in the N–C=O group, confirming the N-doping in the carbon structure.
The N 1s XP spectrum of the ANCS sample shows two peaks at 399.7 and
402.3 eV, which belong to pyrrolic and graphitic nitrogen, respectively
(<a href="javascript:;" reveal-id="fig5" data-open="fig5" class="link link-reveal link-table xref-fig">Figure 5</a>c).  (<a href="javascript:;" ref-data-modal-source-id="ref26" class="link link-ref xref-bibr js-link-ref">26</a>,<a href="javascript:;" ref-data-modal-source-id="ref36" class="link link-ref xref-bibr js-link-ref">36</a>) The pyrrolic nitrogen is dominant in the ANCS sample. The CS sample
shows three O 1s XPS peaks at 530.8, 532.6, and 534.9 eV, corresponding
to oxygen in the C=O, C–O, and O=C–OH groups, respectively
(<a href="javascript:;" reveal-id="fig5" data-open="fig5" class="link link-reveal link-table xref-fig">Figure 5</a>d). (<a href="javascript:;" ref-data-modal-source-id="ref26" class="link link-ref xref-bibr js-link-ref">26</a>) The ACS sample does not contain the third O
1s peak (O=C–OH group), indicating the selective defunctionalization
of acidic groups in carbon by KOH (base). The exposure of the KOH/CS
mixture to the temperature of 900 °C promotes the reaction of
the KOH base with the oxygen-containing acidic functional groups of
CS, resulting in the removal of O=C–OH groups from the carbon
matrix.  (<a href="javascript:;" ref-data-modal-source-id="ref37" class="link link-ref xref-bibr js-link-ref">37</a>,<a href="javascript:;" ref-data-modal-source-id="ref38" class="link link-ref xref-bibr js-link-ref">38</a>) Applying higher temperatures (900 °C)
after the KOH treatment can also remove the oxygen-containing groups
from the carbon matrix in the form of oxygen, carbon dioxide, and
water. (<a href="javascript:;" ref-data-modal-source-id="ref39" class="link link-ref xref-bibr js-link-ref">39</a>) However, the ANCS sample exhibited
the third O 1s peak, corresponding to the O=C–OH group (<a href="javascript:;" reveal-id="fig5" data-open="fig5" class="link link-reveal link-table xref-fig">Figure 5</a>d). This indicates
that KOH activation is not very effective for N-doped CS material
to defunctionalize oxygen species. The reason is that the inserted
N species in the carbon matrix can exhibit a strong electrostatic
interaction with the O=C–OH groups, which are very difficult
to defunctionalize by the KOH treatment.  (<a href="javascript:;" ref-data-modal-source-id="ref40" class="link link-ref xref-bibr js-link-ref">40</a>,<a href="javascript:;" ref-data-modal-source-id="ref41" class="link link-ref xref-bibr js-link-ref">41</a>) The elemental compositions of carbon, nitrogen, oxygen, and hydrogen
in the ANCS catalyst, estimated by CHNO analysis, were found to be
95.78, 1.31, 2.46, and 0.45 wt %, respectively. However, a very low
N amount (0.39 wt %) was found by the XPS analysis as it is a surface-sensitive
technique and cannot detect the total nitrogen amount in the ANCS
catalyst.</p>
                </div>
                <div id="165496542-content" class="article-section-wrapper js-article-section js-content-section  " data-section-parent-id="165496533">
                    
                    <p>The catalyst’s acid sites (Bro̷nsted
and Lewis), especially
Lewis acid sites, play a crucial role in the C–N coupling reactions.  (<a href="javascript:;" ref-data-modal-source-id="ref11" class="link link-ref xref-bibr js-link-ref">11</a>,<a href="javascript:;" ref-data-modal-source-id="ref12" class="link link-ref xref-bibr js-link-ref">12</a>) The electron-deficient carbon in the developed carbon catalysts
can act as the Lewis acid sites (LA), and the hydroxyl and carboxyl
species are the Bro̷nsted acid sites (BA). The pyridine-adsorbed
FT-IR analysis of carbon catalysts is conducted to estimate the nature
of the acid sites (<a href="javascript:;" reveal-id="fig6" data-open="fig6" class="link link-reveal link-table xref-fig">Figure 6</a>). The noticed FT-IR peaks in the range of 1408–1467,
1471–1521, 1524–1554, 1556–1619, and 1645–1699
cm<sup>–1</sup> are assigned to LA, LA + BA, BA, LA, and BA,
respectively (<a href="javascript:;" reveal-id="fig6" data-open="fig6" class="link link-reveal link-table xref-fig">Figure 6</a>).  (<a href="javascript:;" ref-data-modal-source-id="ref11" class="link link-ref xref-bibr js-link-ref">11</a>,<a href="javascript:;" ref-data-modal-source-id="ref42" class="link link-ref xref-bibr js-link-ref">42</a>) The CS sample contains only one peak at
1556–1619 cm<sup>–1</sup>, corresponding to LA sites.
In contrast, the ACS and ANCS samples contain both BA and LA sites’
peaks, but their intensity is lower in the ACS sample compared to
the ANCS sample. It indicates the presence of more Lewis and Bro̷nsted
acid sites in the ANCS catalyst.</p>
                </div>
                <div id="165496543-content" class="article-section-wrapper js-article-section js-content-section  " data-section-parent-id="165496533">
                    
                    <a id = "165496543" scrollto-destination="165496543"></a><div data-id="fig6" class="fig fig-section" reveal-group-id="sec3.1"><span class="hidden" id="viewTranscriptId_"></span><div class="fig-label fig-label">Figure 6</div><div class="graphic-wrap"><a class="fig-link" href="/view-large/figure/165496543/ao4c03987_0006.tif" target="_blank" rel="nofollow" aria-describedby="sr-fig-viewer-action"><img class="content-image js-graphic-position-float" src="https://acs.silverchair-cdn.com/acs/content_public/journal/acsodf/9/33/10.1021_acsomega.4c03987/3/m_ao4c03987_0006.png?Expires=1791347537&amp;Signature=Qj7XFN7XcG3enPe4wcbjBjl78Y9gi60r95NJBs9L2TNo1WkfsdgsDwdGHDhQ4jrSJrwszyUAiumYr7D16zS~4BL3u02RyEWnrwg5YQtQ5HRsnxplTLfgUblVDTRijB1X9PDJBpU81adC~KW~9cUktI7mF~u3jOV244GJxtktC5FDPIy9vTKrlPjf-G1qPRN-I3Ul7YFzILSzBAfuS6MNhnzLoWJHs~3eHhJrJ--LNR2H1zRS31O~joMzwl60mn9bkfLcYR6~U08CRLgVDLKmbn2s5YGuyTH3689fAdEvvGHxxEO3T6kXOODYOUsCdKB8jplj7dAljG9Bw2whufirDw__&amp;Key-Pair-Id=APKAIE5G5CRDK6RD3PGA" data-src="https://acs.silverchair-cdn.com/acs/content_public/journal/acsodf/9/33/10.1021_acsomega.4c03987/3/m_ao4c03987_0006.png?Expires=1791347537&amp;Signature=Qj7XFN7XcG3enPe4wcbjBjl78Y9gi60r95NJBs9L2TNo1WkfsdgsDwdGHDhQ4jrSJrwszyUAiumYr7D16zS~4BL3u02RyEWnrwg5YQtQ5HRsnxplTLfgUblVDTRijB1X9PDJBpU81adC~KW~9cUktI7mF~u3jOV244GJxtktC5FDPIy9vTKrlPjf-G1qPRN-I3Ul7YFzILSzBAfuS6MNhnzLoWJHs~3eHhJrJ--LNR2H1zRS31O~joMzwl60mn9bkfLcYR6~U08CRLgVDLKmbn2s5YGuyTH3689fAdEvvGHxxEO3T6kXOODYOUsCdKB8jplj7dAljG9Bw2whufirDw__&amp;Key-Pair-Id=APKAIE5G5CRDK6RD3PGA" alt="Figure 6. Refer to the image caption for details." path-from-xml="ao4c03987_0006.tif" /></a><div class="fig-orig original-slide"><a section="165496543" class="fig-view-orig at-figureViewLarge" role="button" href="/view-large/figure/165496543/ao4c03987_0006.tif" path-from-xml="ao4c03987_0006.tif" target="_blank" rel="nofollow" aria-label="View large Figure 6.">View Large</a><a section="165496543" href="//acs.silverchair-cdn.com/DownloadFi